continuing the exploration of deities  from Celtic and Irish mythology today  we'll be taking a look at Bridget a  goddess that is commonly associated with  fertility poetry healing Smith crafts  and the season of spring over the years  the goddess went by several names  including Bridget Bridget bridge and  Bragan Shia with her name roughly  translated to mean the exalted one of  the high Wan she does make an appearance  in Irish mythology as a member of the  Tour de Danone the daughter of the Dagda  and the wife of Bres a half form Orion  who once assumed the role of King much  like the Morrigan she was also seen as a  triple goddess being described as the  goddess of poetry with two sisters  sharing the same name Brigid the healer  and Brigid the Smith  she was also thought to be closely  associated with Saint Brigid but not  only shares many of her attributes but  her feast will take place on the 1st of  February the same date as the pagan  festival of amok that would mark the  start of spring this in turn led to many  arguing that Saint Brigid was in fact a  Christianized version of the Irish and  Celtic deity now it became quite common  for Irish and Celtic deities to be  demonized and to be somewhat erased from  existence  when Island and large portions of Europe  became Christianized but this transition  from goddess this ain't allowed Brigid  to survive at a time when any spiritual  belief for the worship of any other gods  outside of Christianity was no longer  being tolerated by European countries  the exact influences of Brigid are  fairly unknown but there has been the  suggestion that she may have been  inspired by an indo-european goddess of  dawn this theory is somewhat further  supported by the goddesses ancient  Gaelic name Brio CJD  men in fiery power indicated to us that  she may have also been a goddess of the  Sun she was often depicted with rays of  light and fire emitting from her hair in  her head showing us that she was the  embodiment of the element of fire Brigid  been a triple goddess may have  contributed the amount of things that  she was considered to be patron of  through her tires were fire in the Sun  she was considered to be the goddess of  Smith crafts the healing arts fertility  poetry music  prophecy and  culture with her name also translated to  mean the high one  she was perceived to be the patron of  relatively high dimensions high rising  flame Highlands and concepts and  activities that elevated oneself  psychologically and physically such as  wisdom high intelligence and druidic  knowledge her association moved the  hearth and the home makes her somewhat  comparable to the goddess Hestia in  greek mythology and this association  continued when she became Saint Brigid  as she was often seen as a motherly  figure the worship of Brigid was fairly  widespread amongst the Celtic people of  Island and stretched the highlands of  Scotland and Western Europe the goddess  performed a vital role unifying warring  clans and providing them with a common  bond many saw bridgid as the fire within  their own heart and the fires of  inspiration playing quite an important  part in the everyday life completely  erasing the goddess from existence was  seen to be a poor decision by the  Catholic Church and so they created a  version of Brigid that they could  integrate into the Catholic religion and  thus the story of Saint Brigid of Kaldur  was started a young woman born into a  pagan family around 450 ad through the  help of st. Patrick she was converted to  Christianity and she was best known for  her generosity compassion and charitable  nature given everything she owned the  way to the poor exploring the story of  Bridget is something that I rather  enjoyed she doesn't have an epic history  full of battle adventure but she's still  a very interesting figure it's fairly  surprising to see the amount of areas  that she was considered the patron of  areas such as craftsmanship and  blacksmithing that commonly would not be  associated to a female figure the  worship of most Celtic and Irish deities  became branded as pagan practice but the  role that Brigid performed was  considered so vital that the Catholic  Church had no other choice but to  integrate her into their religion as a  saint I'd love to hear what you guys  think of Brigid were you familiar with  the transition made from goddess to  Saint do those of you following Wicca  have a different outlook on the goddess  or was some of you just not aware that  she existed let me know in the comments  below as always I've been your host  mythology and fiction explained
 

